<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Priscus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Priscus of Panium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Priscus of Panium was an Eastern Roman diplomat and historian of the 5th century. In 449 he accompanied an embassy to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> and wrote a vivid account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, describing their wooden palaces, banquets and the mixed peoples of the court. His history survives only in fragments but is the main contemporary source on Attila and on the peoples of the steppe in his time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/priscus.jpg" alt="Priscus" width="248" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PriscusofPanium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Priscus of Panium was born in Panion, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, between 410 and 420, and wrote in Greek as a historian and rhetorician. After his journey to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> he spent an interlude in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and then travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. About 456 he appears for the last time in the East, attached to the staff of Euphemios, the magister officiorum of Emperor Marcian. He died after 472.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He accompanied Maximinus, the head of the embassy sent by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>. At the Hunnic court he met a Greek merchant dressed in Hunnic fashion, who had been taken when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viminacium">Viminacium</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> was sacked about 441 or 442. The man had been the slave of the Hunnic nobleman Onegesius, had won his freedom and had chosen to settle among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>. Priscus debated with him the quality of life and justice in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and in the barbarian kingdoms.</p><p>He also described a dinner at one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s houses, built of decorative polished wood with no thought of defence. He brought gifts for Attila's wife, Kreka, who had three sons. The Roman embassy was seated at the end of the table farthest from Attila, who sat on the middle couch with his honoured chiefs on his right. The food came on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold plates, yet Attila used only a wooden cup and dressed plainly. When evening came, torches were lit and songs of his victories were sung.</p><p>The mission itself failed. Unknown to Priscus's party, it served as a respectable front while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> tried to bribe one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s advisers to assassinate him. Attila learned of the plot, and it shaped how he dealt with the ambassadors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Priscus wrote an eight-volume history in Greek, known as the History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, though that was probably not its original title. It probably ran from the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> to the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-emperor">Emperor Zeno</a>, from 433 to 474, but survives only in fragments. Byzantine writers used it heavily, among them Evagrius Scholasticus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus">Cassiodorus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> and the author of the Souda, and it was excerpted in the Excerpta de Legationibus of Emperor Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus (r. 913-959).</p><p>His style is straightforward, and his work is regarded as a reliable contemporary account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, his court and the reception of the Roman ambassadors. He is counted among the classicising historians: although he wrote in the Christian era, his work is almost completely secular and follows an historiographical tradition reaching back to the fifth century BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Priscus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maurice's generals, notably <b>Priscus</b>, campaigned north of the Danube in the 590s and defeated the Avars near Viminacium and on the Tisza in 599, but the emperor's overthrow in 602 ended the counter-offensive.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Elites practised polygamy, commoners probably monogamy; <b>Priscus</b> saw women moving freely among men, and Bleda's widow commanded a village through which Roman envoys rode.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Maurice's Balkan campaigns, the Avars lifted their siege of <b>Priscus</b>' army at Tomis as Comentiolus led a Roman army along the Danube.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-30">30 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Akatziri are first mentioned by the Byzantine envoy <b>Priscus</b> in the 440s as a people of the Pontic steppe ruled by many chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/state/akatziri">Akatziri</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sabirs moved west from the steppes of Western Siberia around 463, when, according to <b>Priscus</b>, they were pushed by the Avars and in turn drove the Saragurs, Urogs and Onogurs towards the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/sabirs">Sabirs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Panion, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.91,27.47&amp;year=464" aria-label="Panion on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5222 703 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Priscus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5672 973h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5672 973h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.91,27.47&amp;year=464">What was here in 464 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
